# Bank 3 (runs at 0xA000). Directives: L label, C line comment,
# B block header, D data range, F row width.
#
# The code of bank 2 falls through from 0x9FFF to 0xA000 and comes in here
# without any jump: the two banks are always mapped together.
#
# AND AT THE END OF THE BANK IS KONAMI'S HIDDEN MARK (0xBFF5-0xBFFF). It is
# not our find: Manuel Pazos (@ManuelPazosMSX) uncovered it in 2021.
# Behind the 0xFF filler, and reading towards the end, there is [title
# backwards] [how many characters] [last two digits of the RC in BCD] [0xAA].
# Here it gives 8 characters and RC-742, and the title in katakana is
# グラディウス, that is, GRADIUS. tools/konami_mark.py extracts it.
B 0xA000 THE BANK STARTS WHERE BANK 2 ENDS, WITHOUT ANY JUMP
L 0xA000 continued_from_bank_2   The code coming from 0x9FFF carries on here with no instruction in between
C 0xA000 The code coming from 0x9FFF comes in here
C 0xA006 The cell, erased
C 0xA00B And whatever is left of the strip
L 0xA00F laser_cut_short   The laser hits the map: its length is cut and, if nothing is left, it goes out
C 0xA00F Step 2: the laser no longer grows
C 0xA013 Byte 12: how many cells long it is
C 0xA018 With no cells left, the laser goes out
B 0xA022 THE POWER-UP METER
B 0xA022 The six slots of the meter (SPEED UP, MISSILE, DOUBLE, LASER, OPTION and
B 0xA022 the shield) are painted lit or unlit according to what the ship already
B 0xA022 carries. 0xA022 walks the ship's card and leaves in 0xE131..0xE136 a one
B 0xA022 for each upgrade already taken; 0xA068 looks at 0xE130 (the selected
B 0xA022 slot) and, when the button is pressed, gives that upgrade if it is not
B 0xA022 there yet.
L 0xA022 note_upgrades_held   Leaves in 0xE131 to 0xE136 which upgrades the ship already has, to paint the meter
C 0xA02D 0xE136: the shield
C 0xA033 Byte 2 above 8: it already has speed
C 0xA039 0xE131: the speed
C 0xA041 Byte 11 at two or more: it already has the missile
C 0xA047 0xE135: the missile
C 0xA04D 0xE133: the double shot
C 0xA052 Byte 13 at two or more: it already has the laser
C 0xA058 0xE134: the laser
C 0xA05D And byte 14: the options
L 0xA068 take_upgrade   With the button, if the selected slot of the meter is not already taken, the ship gets it and sound 0x14 plays
C 0xA06D Bit 5 of what was just pressed: the other button
C 0xA073 0xE130: the selected slot
C 0xA07F If that upgrade is already there, nothing is done
C 0xA083 The meter goes back to zero
C 0xA08A Sound 0x14
D 0xA094 0xA0A0 dispatcher_table_A091  Six words stuck right after the `call 0x4067` at 0xA091 (0xA0A0, 0xA0A5, 0xA0AC, 0xA0B7, 0xA0C1, 0xA0CB).
F 0xA094 w2
L 0xA0A0 upgrade_speed   One more notch of speed
L 0xA0A5 upgrade_missile   Raises the missile and raises the difficulty
L 0xA0AC upgrade_double   Sets the double shot and removes the laser
C 0xA0AC 0xE20E to zero: no more laser
C 0xA0B3 And 0xE20C and 0xE20D to one: the double is on
L 0xA0B7 upgrade_laser   Sets the laser and removes the double
C 0xA0B7 0xE20C and 0xE20D to zero: no more double
C 0xA0BF And 0xE20E up: the laser is on
L 0xA0C1 upgrade_option   One more option and the difficulty goes up
L 0xA0CB upgrade_shield   0xE200 to 3 and 0xE201 to 0x0A: the shield is on
C 0xA0CB 0xE200 to three and 0xE201 to 0x0A
C 0xA0D3 And 0x70CA raises the difficulty
B 0xA0D8 WHAT EACH CHEAT GIVES
B 0xA0D8 This is where the cheats typed on the keyboard land, dispatched by 0x50C9
B 0xA0D8 in bank 0. Each one sets one field of the ship and returns:
B 0xA0D8   0xA0D8  HYPER and the stage's woman's name: all FIVE at once
B 0xA0D8   0xA0E9  SHIELD    0xA101  LASER     0xA106  MISSILE
B 0xA0D8   0xA10B  DOUBLE    0xA110  OPTION    0xA0FA  DOWN, which takes away
L 0xA0D8 cheat_everything   HYPER and the stage's name: shield, speed, laser, missile and option, all five in a row
C 0xA0D8 All five at once: shield...
C 0xA0DB ...speed, laser, missile...
C 0xA0E4 ...and one option
L 0xA0E9 cheat_shield   Only the shield
L 0xA0EE dead_code_A0EE  Code that nothing reaches.
D 0xA0EE 0xA0FA dead_code_A0EE  Twelve bytes that disassemble as code (`ld hl,0xE202 / ld a,(hl) / cp 7 / ret nc / call 0xA11E / jr`), but that no path reaches: the instruction before them is a `jr` and no table points here.
F 0xA0EE 12
L 0xA0FA cheat_down   0xE202 to zero: takes away from the ship what 0xA123 raises
L 0xA101 cheat_laser   Only the laser
L 0xA106 cheat_missile   Only the missile
L 0xA10B cheat_double   Only the double shot
L 0xA110 cheat_option   Only the options, up to the two that fit
L 0xA115 set_shield   0xE200 to 3 and 0xE201 to 0x0A: the shield is on
D 0xA11E 0xA123 dead_code_A11E  Five bytes that are `ld hl,0xE202 / inc (hl) / ret`. The only caller is the dead piece at 0xA0EE, so they never run either.
F 0xA11E 5
L 0xA123 raise_speed   0xE202 to one
L 0xA129 set_laser   0xE20C and 0xE20D to zero and 0xE20E to two: the shot becomes the laser
C 0xA129 0xE20C and 0xE20D to zero...
C 0xA131 ...and 0xE20E to two: the laser
L 0xA134 set_missile   0xE20F to two
L 0xA13A set_double_shot   0xE20C and 0xE20D to one, and 0xE20E to zero
C 0xA13A 0xE20C and 0xE20D to one...
C 0xA144 ...and 0xE20E to zero: the double
L 0xA146 add_option   Raises 0xE20B up to two and, for each one, calls 0x9BFB
C 0xA14A Two options at most
L 0xA153 refresh_meter   Notes again what the ship carries and repaints the meter
L 0xA159 upload_four_empty_cards   Four switched-off cards: the ship is not there
L 0xA162 upload_explosion_card   With the ship dead, the cards come from somewhere else
C 0xA162 With the ship blown up, the card comes from four bytes further on
C 0xA166 The row and the column of the explosion...
C 0xA16F ...and the rest of the card, from the ship
C 0xA179 The drawings and the colours
L 0xA17F upload_ship_cards   Copies to the sprite buffer the cards of the ship, its two options and its shots
C 0xA17F 0xEC80: the sprite attribute buffer
C 0xA185 With 0xE200 at zero there is no ship, and with bit 7 it is blown up
C 0xA19E With the ship in state 1, the card goes as it is
C 0xA1A5 And otherwise, eight points lower
C 0xA1B7 The two options, 0xE220 and 0xE240
C 0xA1C3 And the shot tables: 0xE270, 0xE290, 0xE2B0...
C 0xA1D8 Three missiles
L 0xA1DA upload_three_missiles   The three missiles at 0xE2C0; an empty slot is switched off
C 0xA1DA Three missiles, eight bytes apart
C 0xA1E0 No missile, card switched off
C 0xA1EA Eight bytes: the next one
L 0xA1F2 missile_off   Empty card and on to the next one
C 0xA1F5 Sixteen bytes: the next one
L 0xA1FD upload_option_card   If the option is there, its position, pattern and colour to the buffer
C 0xA1FD With the option on, its row, its column, its drawing and its colour
C 0xA207 Four bytes of card
L 0xA213 upload_shot_card   Only those of type 2 (the double) upload a sprite card
C 0xA213 Those of type 2 (the double) are the only ones that upload a card
C 0xA21B Its row and its column
L 0xA225 card_off   0xE0 in Y: the VDP does not draw it
C 0xA225 0xE0 in the row: the VDP does not draw the sprite
C 0xA229 And the other three bytes, to zero
B 0xA22D THE LASER IS PAINTED IN THE NAME TABLE, NOT WITH SPRITES
B 0xA22D The normal shot is a sprite, but the laser is not: it is a STRIP OF
B 0xA22D CHARACTERS written into the map. 0xA22D saves what lies underneath (its
B 0xA22D cell and its length) and then writes the strip; 0xA27F erases it by
B 0xA22D putting the characters back. That is why the laser can cover the terrain
B 0xA22D as it goes by.
L 0xA22D save_under_laser   Walks the six slots and, for the laser, notes where it starts and how many cells it covers
C 0xA231 Six slots
C 0xA236 Type 1: the normal shot
C 0xA239 And 3: the laser
C 0xA23E Sixteen bytes: the next one
L 0xA247 save_shot_cell   The cell it lands on, and the character that was there
C 0xA247 The cell where the shot lands...
C 0xA24D ...and the character that was there
L 0xA251 save_laser_strip   Notes the cell in bytes 14 and 15 and copies the strip of characters it is about to cover
C 0xA254 Byte 12: how many cells long it is
C 0xA26D What was underneath is copied
L 0xA272 shot_cell   From the shot's position comes its cell in the map
C 0xA272 From the row and the column comes the map cell
C 0xA279 0x571B: the cell that belongs to it
L 0xA27F write_laser   Writes the shot's character into the map, and for the laser its whole strip
C 0xA283 Six slots
C 0xA285 Byte 1 says what each slot holds
C 0xA290 Sixteen bytes: the next one
L 0xA299 write_shot   A single character in its cell
C 0xA299 The shot's row and column
C 0xA2A1 From that comes its cell in the map
C 0xA2A6 And there its character is written
L 0xA2AA write_laser_strip   Repeats the laser character over as many cells as it is long
C 0xA2AD Byte 12: how many cells
C 0xA2B3 Six bytes further on: the character
C 0xA2BB Bytes 14 and 15: where the strip starts
C 0xA2C1 The same character, that many times
L 0xA2C7 restore_underneath   Erases the shot from the map by putting back the character it had saved, and for the laser its whole strip
C 0xA2CB Six slots
C 0xA2D0 Type 1: the normal shot
C 0xA2D3 And 3: the laser
C 0xA2D8 Sixteen bytes: the next one
L 0xA2E1 restore_shot_cell   The saved character goes back to its cell
C 0xA2E1 The cell that had been noted...
C 0xA2E9 ...and the character that was underneath
C 0xA2F0 Back in its place
L 0xA2F4 restore_laser_strip   Copies back the whole strip the laser was covering
C 0xA2F7 Byte 12: how many cells
C 0xA2FD Bytes 14 and 15: where it started
B 0xA310 WHICH ENEMIES COME OUT: ONE BYTE OF BITS PER STRETCH OF SCREEN
B 0xA310 The distance travelled, divided by 0x20, indexes a list per stage
B 0xA310 (0xA3A6): out comes ONE BYTE whose six low bits say which groups of
B 0xA310 enemies are active in that stretch. 0xA359 rotates it bit by bit and
B 0xA310 calls the routine of every group that is switched on. That way each piece
B 0xA310 of the stage has its own mix of enemies without needing a long script.
L 0xA310 release_stretch_enemies   With the stretch's byte of bits, calls the routine of every enemy group that is switched on
C 0xA310 0xE129: the frames left until the next wave
C 0xA319 With the screen stopped, no
C 0xA31E From the ninth stage onwards, not either
C 0xA324 Nor with a wave already under way
C 0xA329 Nor with 0xE064 at two or more
C 0xA32F With the boss on screen they only come out on some steps
C 0xA348 0xE124: whether one has just come out
C 0xA351 With eleven objects minus the live ones below 0xE162, it waits
C 0xA359 The stretch's byte of bits
C 0xA35C Bit 0: the first group
C 0xA362 Bit 1
C 0xA368 Bit 2
C 0xA36E Bit 3
C 0xA374 Bit 4
C 0xA37A And bit 5
C 0xA37E And after them, the five mover routines that always run
L 0xA38D stretch_bits   The distance divided by 0x20 indexes the stage's list and returns the byte of bits
C 0xA390 The table at 0xA3A6: one list per stage
C 0xA396 The distance travelled
C 0xA39A Divided by 0x20: the stretch
D 0xA3A6 0xA438 table_A3A6  Words read by 0xA390 with base 0xA3A6 (0xA3B8, 0xA3C8, 0xA3D8, ...) and, after them, what they point to.
F 0xA3A6 w2
L 0xA438 release_pair   Every 0x70 frames minus twice the difficulty, releases two type 3 enemies, one along row 0x30 and the other along 0x60
C 0xA438 0xE96C: the frames left
C 0xA441 With eleven or more live objects, it is left for later
C 0xA447 The wait for the next pair is reloaded
C 0xA44F With the adjustment at two, each one gets its own
C 0xA453 The R register: the cartridge's only coin toss
C 0xA459 One gets 1 and the other 2, or the other way round
C 0xA45E X 0xF0 and Y 0x30: comes in from the right, at the top
C 0xA467 And the second along 0x60, lower down
L 0xA46F release_trail   A line of type 4 enemies, one every eight frames, coming in at four heights that take turns
C 0xA46F 0xE960: how many are still to come out
C 0xA476 0xE961: the frames until the next one
C 0xA479 Eight frames between one enemy and the next
C 0xA47C One fewer to come out
C 0xA47E The two low bits of the count: the height goes by turns
C 0xA487 Always from the right
L 0xA490 start_another_trail   The line is over: the difficulty says how many the next one brings
C 0xA490 0xE111, the difficulty, indexes the ramp at 0xA4AA
L 0xA49C wait_by_difficulty   0x70 minus twice the difficulty: the harder the stage, the shorter the wait
C 0xA49F The difficulty times two, subtracted from 0x70
D 0xA4A6 0xA4AA table_A4A6  Four bytes (0x20, 0x80, 0x40, 0x70) read by 0xA480.
F 0xA4A6 4
D 0xA4AA 0xA4BA ramp_A4AA  Sixteen bytes in a ramp, read by 0xA493 with `ld de,0xA4AA`.
F 0xA4AA 16
B 0xA4BA THE WAVE THAT COMES IN SINGLE FILE
B 0xA4BA Six enemies of the same type coming in single file from the right. The
B 0xA4BA wave is noted in one of the four group cards at 0xE900 with its count,
B 0xA4BA and p00:0x5E65 brings it down as they fall; when the last one falls, the
B 0xA4BA group closes and that last one blows up with a different drawing. The
B 0xA4BA stage decides which type comes out: the byte at 0xA5AF carries TWO
B 0xA4BA types, one in each half, and they alternate from wave to wave.
L 0xA4BA run_wave   The three steps of the wave: not started, waiting for room, and releasing an enemy every few frames
C 0xA4BA 0xE160: which step the wave is on
C 0xA4C1 Step 1: waiting for there to be room
C 0xA4C4 0xE161: the frames until the next one
C 0xA4C7 0xE163, the type, says how often they come out
C 0xA4CC Type 2 goes every four
C 0xA4D2 And 0x0C, every sixteen
C 0xA4D8 The rest go in pairs: four and one, four and one
C 0xA4E1 The wait, reloaded
C 0xA4E3 One fewer of the six
C 0xA4E8 And with the last one out, the wave is over
C 0xA4EA 0xE165: the height at which the whole wave comes in
C 0xA4EE X 0xF0: through the right edge
C 0xA4F8 0xE124 stays at zero if there was no free slot
C 0xA4FD No room: the enemy goes back into the count...
C 0xA503 ...and the wave goes back to step 1
L 0xA506 wait_for_room   With seven live objects or fewer, the wave starts
L 0xA50E set_up_wave   Every eight steps of the stage sets up a new wave, with its type, its height and its group card
C 0xA50E Only on the steps that put in a column
C 0xA513 The distance travelled
C 0xA516 Waves only come out before step 0x80...
C 0xA520 ...and in the eighth stage, before 0x40
C 0xA524 One wave every eight steps
C 0xA528 0xE160 to one and 0xE161 to one: the wave starts
C 0xA52E The stage indexes the list at 0xA5AF
C 0xA53A 0xE166 goes up by one per wave, skipping zero
C 0xA543 Its bit 0 picks the half: one wave of each type
C 0xA54C The type of this wave, to 0xE163
C 0xA552 The two low bits: 0xE164 to zero or to one
C 0xA55C The height at which they come in
C 0xA562 Six enemies per wave
C 0xA567 A free group card is looked for at 0xE900
C 0xA56D No free card, no wave
L 0xA572 note_group   The card at 0xE900 keeps the wave number and, twice, the six it brings
C 0xA572 The wave number, in the group card
C 0xA577 And the six it brings, twice
L 0xA57E wave_height   Where it comes in: top or bottom for almost all, and types 0x0A and 0x0C have their own
C 0xA57F Type 0x0A comes in at 0x40 or at 0x80
C 0xA583 And 0x0C, at 6 or at 0x98
C 0xA587 The two halves of the stage's byte...
C 0xA592 ...and if they are equal, another bit is checked
C 0xA598 Row 8: at the top
C 0xA59D Or 0x98: at the bottom
D 0xA5AF 0xA5B8 table_A5AF  Nine bytes read by 0xA531 with base 0xA5AF.
F 0xA5AF 9
B 0xA5B8 ONE IN FOUR COMES OUT MARKED
B 0xA5B8 The adjustment passed to spawn_object is the enemy's mark: with it at
B 0xA5B8 zero the enemy comes out like the rest, and when it is non-zero
B 0xA5B8 p01:0x6B0D gives it colour 8 (the red one) and p00:0x5E88 changes what
B 0xA5B8 it leaves behind when it dies. The table at 0xA5C7 hands it out round and
B 0xA5B8 round: three at zero and one marked, and once in every sixteen the mark
B 0xA5B8 is 2 instead of 1.
L 0xA5B8 mark_next_or_not   A counter going round the table at 0xA5C7: three enemies out of four come out unmarked
C 0xA5B8 0xE125: how many enemies have been requested
C 0xA5BC The four low bits: sixteen entries
C 0xA5BE And one more for the next one
D 0xA5C7 0xA5D7 table_A5C7  Sixteen bytes read by 0xA5BF, in groups of four.
F 0xA5C7 4
L 0xA5D7 release_flock   Eight type 9 enemies, one every seven frames: the first four along the bottom and the last four along the top
C 0xA5D7 0xE962: how many are left of the flock
C 0xA5DF 0xE963: the frames until the next one
C 0xA5E2 Seven frames between one enemy and the next
C 0xA5E5 One fewer of the eight
C 0xA5EA With the flock out, 0x3C frames until the next one
C 0xA5ED From four onwards...
C 0xA5F0 ...row 0x88, along the bottom
C 0xA5F5 And the last four along 0x18
C 0xA5F7 All eight come in from the right
L 0xA600 wait_for_flock   When the countdown runs out, the next flock is set up with eight
C 0xA600 0xE963: the frames until the next flock
C 0xA60A Eight per flock
L 0xA60D release_left_ones   Eight type 5 enemies that come in through the LEFT edge, taking turns between row 8 and 0x90
C 0xA60D 0xE966: how many are left
C 0xA615 0xE967: the frames until the next one
C 0xA618 The difficulty times four, subtracted from 0x70: the wait
C 0xA623 One fewer
C 0xA625 The three low bits: the row goes by turns
C 0xA630 X at zero: these come in from the LEFT
C 0xA632 With its mark, if it gets one
L 0xA63A set_up_left_ones   Another eight, and the first one on the next frame
D 0xA640 0xA648 table_A640  Eight bytes read by 0xA627.
F 0xA640 8
L 0xA648 release_bottom_four   Four type 6 enemies along row 0x8F: the first three from the right and the last one from the left
C 0xA648 0xE96A: how many are left of the four
C 0xA650 0xE96B: the frames until the next one
C 0xA653 The difficulty times four, subtracted from 0x60: the wait
C 0xA65E One fewer
C 0xA662 All along row 0x8F
C 0xA664 From the right...
C 0xA669 ...except the last one, which comes in from the left
C 0xA66B With its mark, if it gets one
L 0xA673 set_up_bottom_four   Another four, and the first one on the next frame
L 0xA679 release_mixed_eight   Eight enemies: the first four of type 5 on the side opposite the ship, and the last four of type 6 alternating sides
C 0xA679 0xE96A: the same count as 0xA648
C 0xA684 The difficulty times four, subtracted from 0x50
C 0xA691 The first four go elsewhere
C 0xA695 Bit 0 of the count: one side and then the other
C 0xA69D The type 6 ones, along row 0x90
L 0xA6A6 enter_opposite_side   Looks at which half the ship is in and releases the enemy through the other one
C 0xA6A6 0xE204: the ship's row
C 0xA6A9 Above the middle of the screen...
C 0xA6AF ...the enemy comes in at the top, and otherwise at the bottom
C 0xA6B1 The type 5 ones, always from the left
L 0xA6BA set_up_mixed_eight   Another eight, and the first one on the next frame
B 0xA6C0 WHEN THE BOSS DIES, THE SCREEN GOES DARK BY EATING THE VRAM
B 0xA6C0 The cartridge does not clear the screen to end the stage: it eats it. It
B 0xA6C0 brings blocks of 0x200 bytes down from VRAM to 0xEA00, ANDs them with a
B 0xA6C0 mask and sends them back up. First it makes one pass over the COLOUR
B 0xA6C0 table (which in this cartridge lives at VRAM 0x0000) with mask 0xF0, and
B 0xA6C0 then repeats the pass EIGHT times over the PATTERN table (0x2000) with
B 0xA6C0 0xFE, 0xFC, 0xF8... down to 0x00, also rotating the mask three bits per
B 0xA6C0 byte, so that the black comes in crumbled and not in rows.
B 0xA6C0 Bank 2 calls it from two different bosses, and when it is done it sets
B 0xA6C0 0xE1A3.
L 0xA6C0 switch_off_screen   The four steps of the fade: eat the colours, eat the patterns, clear 0xED00 and report back
C 0xA6C0 0xE1A0: which step the fade is on
C 0xA6CE If nothing else is already playing, sound 0x3E
C 0xA6D7 0xE1A3 to zero: not finished yet
C 0xA6DB 0xE1A1 to zero: starting from the first row
C 0xA6DE 0xE1A2: the mask starts at 0xF0
L 0xA6E0 eat_background_colours   A whole pass over the colour table, 0x40 rows at a time
C 0xA6E0 0xE1A1: which row it is on
C 0xA6E3 The first third does not reach 0x1000
C 0xA6ED The third third of colours
C 0xA6F3 The second...
C 0xA6F9 ...and the first
C 0xA703 0x40 more rows
C 0xA709 Once all the way round, step 1
C 0xA70C And the pattern mask starts at 0xFE
L 0xA70F eat_patterns   The same pass, but over the pattern table and removing one more bit each round
C 0xA712 Below row 0x40, only two thirds
C 0xA716 And row 0x44 takes fewer bytes
C 0xA71C The third third of patterns
C 0xA722 The second...
C 0xA728 ...and the first
C 0xA735 Until it has gone all the way round, nothing
C 0xA73B One bit fewer in the mask
C 0xA73E With the mask at zero everything is black: step 2
L 0xA744 clear_buffer   The 0x2C0 bytes of 0xED00 to zero, and on to step 3
C 0xA744 0xED00 onwards...
C 0xA74A ...0x2C0 bytes to zero
C 0xA751 And step 3
L 0xA757 report_done   When the sound stops, 0xE1A3 is set and bank 2 takes the boss as dead
C 0xA757 0xE012: not until the sound stops
C 0xA75C 0xE1A3 to one: bank 2 can now carry on
L 0xA762 chunk_with_current_mask   Comes in with the current row in 0xE1A1
L 0xA765 eat_chunk   Brings 0x200 bytes down from VRAM to 0xEA00, applies the mask to them and sends them back up
C 0xA765 Half a kilobyte at a time
C 0xA768 Two rows take fewer: 0x44...
C 0xA76F ...and 0xC0
C 0xA776 The row times eight: the byte where it starts
C 0xA77E 0xEA00: the block comes down here
C 0xA78A 0xE1A2: this round's mask
C 0xA796 And the block, bitten, goes back to VRAM
L 0xA799 apply_mask   In the first step the mask is the same for every byte; in the rest it rotates three bits per byte
L 0xA79F fixed_mask   AND with the same mask byte by byte
C 0xA79F AND with the mask, byte by byte
C 0xA7A3 Half a kilobyte
L 0xA7A9 rotating_mask   Three bits of rotation per byte: the black comes in crumbled
C 0xA7A9 The same mask, rotated three bits...
C 0xA7B3 ...on every byte of the block
B 0xA7B9 EACH ENEMY TYPE HAS TWO ROUTINES, AND ALMOST ALL OF THEM ARE HERE
B 0xA7B9 An object boils down to one number, its type, from 1 to 0x1F. That
B 0xA7B9 number drives two tables: the one at p00:0x5DFD, which says who moves it
B 0xA7B9 every frame, and the one at p01:0x6B46, which says who finishes building
B 0xA7B9 it when it is born. Of the thirty-one types, TWENTY have at least one of
B 0xA7B9 their two routines here, and SIXTEEN have both; that is why
B 0xA7B9 0xA7B9..0xB537 is the longest stretch of code in the cartridge.
L 0xA7B9 finish_type_16   No speed and bit 1 of byte 27 cleared
L 0xA7C0 finish_type_17   The same, and byte 23 to one
L 0xA7CB finish_type_18   The same, and byte 23 to eight
L 0xA7D6 finish_type_19   Only the speeds to zero
L 0xA7D9 move_types_17_and_18   They move with the scroll and, when they go off screen, break the capsule chain
L 0xA7DF move_type_1A   Moves with the scroll and, when its count runs out, turns into the type noted in byte 24
C 0xA7DF Moves with the scroll
C 0xA7E2 Going off the edge is handled elsewhere
C 0xA7EF Byte 2: the frames it has left like this
C 0xA7F3 Byte 24 says what it turns into
C 0xA7F6 With 0x16 it does not turn into anything: it switches off
C 0xA7FB And byte 23 counts how many times it can
C 0xA801 The new type, in its place
C 0xA804 Byte 11 to one: drawn with characters
C 0xA808 And character 0x38
C 0xA810 Byte 25 reloads the count
L 0xA817 left_unclaimed   When it leaves through the edge, the capsule chain starts over
C 0xA81D 0xE128 to zero: the next capsule pays one point again
L 0xA821 finish_type_3   Down four, two to the left, and its path curves
C 0xA821 Four points per frame downwards
C 0xA827 And that same figure as horizontal acceleration
C 0xA82A Two to the left
C 0xA830 With half a point of vertical acceleration
