B 0xBA9D THE SPIRAL USES NO SINES: IT IS DONE WITH AN EIGHTH AND A MULTIPLICATION
B 0xBA9D Turning around a centre comes here from a three-line rule: take the
B 0xBA9D difference to the centre along one direction, divide it by eight (three
B 0xBA9D `sra a`) and add it to the other; and with the other, the other way
B 0xBA9D round. That gives a turn of about seven degrees per step, without
B 0xBA9D touching any sine table. The radius shrinks separately: both coordinates
B 0xBA9D are multiplied by byte 20, which starts at 0xFF and drops by one every
B 0xBA9D 0x3C frames, so the loop closes on its own. Bit 0 of byte 30 picks the
B 0xBA9D direction: the two pieces are the same with the signs swapped.
