C 0xBCE3 The second and third slots do not move on their own
C 0xBCE8 Byte 2: the frames of life
C 0xBCF0 With 0x1E to go, it starts blinking its colour
C 0xBCF5 The drawing, which goes its own way
C 0xBCFB The ship's row minus its own
C 0xBD01 A third of a point towards it
C 0xBD0F Not past row 0x70...
C 0xBD1B ...nor above 0x10
C 0xBD25 And the other two, behind
C 0xBD2D Past column 0xD0, it is turned round
L 0xBD33 type_1E_splits_in_three   Once the count is over, the three slots become type 0x1F and fly off, each in its own direction
C 0xBD33 The table at 0xBD71: two speeds per piece
C 0xBD37 The three slots
C 0xBD3A Type 0x1F and character 0xE8
C 0xBD63 0x20 bytes: the next slot
C 0xBD6B And back to the first
D 0xBD71 0xBD7D table_BD71  Twelve bytes read by 0xBD33, as signed words.
F 0xBD71 w2
L 0xBD7D move_type_1F   Blows up when it touches the map, blinks, and chases the ship or heads for row 0x58 depending on the big ones' flock
C 0xBD80 Touching the map, it blows up
C 0xBD83 The blinking
C 0xBD86 0xE990: with the big ones' flock under way, it chases
C 0xBD8C The acceleration towards the ship, and the two sums
C 0xBD95 And otherwise, it heads for row 0x58
C 0xBD9A Two points downwards...
C 0xBD9F ...or two upwards
L 0xBDA5 type_1F_touches_map   Checks the cell where it is and, if it is descending, the one 0x10 lower
C 0xBDA5 Its row and its column
C 0xBDAB Byte 8: when descending, it looks lower
L 0xBDB8 blink_type_1F   Flips bit 2 of its character every 10 or every 3 frames, drawn by lot with the R register
C 0xBDBE The R register: 10 frames or only 3
C 0xBDCC Bit 2 of the character: two drawings
B 0xBDD2 THE BACKGROUND THAT MOVES WITHOUT SPENDING A SINGLE SPRITE
B 0xBDD2 No object moves here: what changes is the DRAWING of three characters
B 0xBDD2 (0xF6, 0xF7 and 0xF8), by rewriting their eight bytes in VRAM. The table
B 0xBDD2 at 0xBE19 holds six drawings for each of them, and each one goes its own
B 0xBDD2 way: how long its drawing lasts is drawn by lot by the R register,
B 0xBDD2 between 8 and 0x17 frames. Since the background is made with those three
B 0xBDD2 characters, all the cells that use them move at once at no cost.
L 0xBDD2 animate_three_characters   Rewrites in VRAM the eight bytes of characters 0xF6, 0xF7 and 0xF8
C 0xBDD5 Character 0xF6, with its count at 0xE700
C 0xBDE0 0xF7, with its own at 0xE702
C 0xBDEB And 0xF8, at 0xE704
L 0xBDF3 animate_character   When the count runs out, moves on to the next of the six drawings and uploads it to VRAM
C 0xBDF3 The frames this drawing has left
C 0xBDF5 The R register: between 8 and 0x17 frames
C 0xBDFD The next drawing...
C 0xBDFF ...of the six, round and round
C 0xBE09 The character number times eight: where it lives in VRAM
C 0xBE13 Eight bytes: one row of characters
D 0xBE19 0xBE2B table_BE19  Eighteen bytes read by 0xBDD8 (0xBE19), 0xBDE3 (0xBE1F) and 0xBDEE (0xBE25), in groups of six.
F 0xBE19 6
B 0xBE2B AND SEVEN CELLS THAT CHANGE CHARACTER
B 0xBE2B The other background trick: seven eight-byte cards at 0xE710, each one
B 0xBE2B with the VRAM address of a map cell. Every so many frames another
B 0xBE2B character from the list 0xF0, 0xF2, 0xF4, 0xF2 is written over it, and
B 0xBE2B the next one over the cell below (0x20 bytes further on). Two cells per
B 0xBE2B card, and not a single object involved.
L 0xBE2B animate_seven_cells   Seven eight-byte cards at 0xE710, each one with its map cell
C 0xBE2B Seven cards
C 0xBE37 Eight bytes per card
L 0xBE3E animate_cell   Writes into its cell the character that is due, and the next one into the cell below
C 0xBE3E The frames it has left
C 0xBE41 Reloaded from the byte next to it
C 0xBE46 The next drawing, four of them round and round
C 0xBE4B The list at 0xBE62
C 0xBE53 The VRAM address of its cell
C 0xBE5A And 0x20 further on, the one below
D 0xBE62 0xBE66 table_BE62  Four bytes (0xF0, 0xF2, 0xF4, 0xF2) read by 0xBE4B with `ld de,0xBE62`.
F 0xBE62 4
D 0xBE66 0xBFF5 filler  0xFF filler: the gap left between the bank's last data and the Konami mark.
F 0xBE66 16
B 0xBFF5 THE MARK KONAMI HID AT THE END OF THE BANK
D 0xBFF5 0xC000 konami_mark  The mark Konami hid at the end of the bank. Eleven bytes: the eight of the title BACKWARDS (8C 82 B4 B7 92 A6 B7 87), the 0x08 that says how many there are, the 0x42 that is the last two digits of the RC in BCD (that is, RC-742) and the 0xAA that closes it. Read forwards and with the house katakana table (index = byte minus 0x80, gojuon in order) they give グラディウス: GRADIUS. The find is Manuel Pazos's (@ManuelPazosMSX).
F 0xBFF5 8,1,1,1
